WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.510
ABHI YADAV: Thank you
all for joining us

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:09.240
on our talk today, in which we
take you through a deep dive

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.820
of Google Cloud's
IAM Recommender.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.620
And we will walk
you through how Uber

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.200
was successful in
minimizing permissions

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.940
using this solution.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.730
Sonal and the Uber team provided
us with valuable insights

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:22.260
during early stages.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:24.150
And they'll be
sharing Uber's journey

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.790
and improving IAM for
the cloud in mind.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:28.200
Hi, Sonal.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:29.520
SONAL DESAI: Thank you, Abhi.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:30.430
Hello, everyone.

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:31.530
My name is Sonal.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.600
And I am a security
engineer at Uber.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.660
I'm thrilled to be
here today to talk

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.240
about how IAM
Recommender helped us

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:43.110
identify a significant number
of over-granted permissions

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:46.080
in our GCP
infrastructure and helped

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.983
us bring closer to our goal
of achieving least privilege.

00:00:50.983 --> 00:00:52.400
ABHI YADAV: OK,
let's get started.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.460
So today, we're going to talk
a lot about IAM policies.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:58.030
So let's first start
with a quick refresher

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:01.360
for those who might not be too
familiar with an anatomy of IAM

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:02.670
policy.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:04.849
So policies as
shown here today are

00:01:04.849 --> 00:01:08.090
a list of bindings
of rules to members.

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:09.650
Rules contain permissions.

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:12.470
And it can be referenced
once in the policy.

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:15.380
Members can include groups,
users, service accounts,

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:16.300
and more.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.380
Here, things can
get a bit complex

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:22.020
if the group contains
nested group as members.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.140
The policy also
contains an audit config

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:26.300
that determines which
permission types are logged.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:29.790
A policy document is attached
or applied at a resource.

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:32.180
For example, it can be
applied on a project

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:36.470
and then inherits downward all
the resources in that project.

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:39.620
So effective access for
a member is a combination

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:42.290
of the roles in this
policy and a union

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:46.390
of all of those inherited from
above, like from a folder.

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:49.500
Now, policy evaluation always
traverses the hierarchy tree

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:50.720
downward.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.510
This is why in some cases,
managing project-level policies

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:55.600
is recommended.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:59.090
Lastly, remember that setting
policy programmatically

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:01.850
replaces any existing
policy on the resource.

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:04.730
So think about it as a whole,
and not just the delta,

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:09.680
to avoid removing any old
content that was already there.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:12.290
Now, assuming you
understand that well,

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:14.870
let's think about
whether your policies are

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:19.090
constructed accurately to
reduce security risk exposure.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:22.940
Ideally, you want to reach
a state of least privilege,

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:24.400
where you limit
the attack surface

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.670
by granting users only the
access or the permissions

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:29.690
they need.

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:33.170
But we found that, when
it comes to risk exposure,

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:36.170
people tend to underestimate
the risk they are in.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:38.622
It's only human nature.

00:02:38.622 --> 00:02:40.080
And while digging
into this problem

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.990
space with our customers,
we found that consistently,

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:46.740
across the board, admins were
granting way more permissions

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.090
than the users needed.

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:51.040
One reason was to
ensure that developers

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.510
could do what they want,
but also because changing

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.720
policies is complex and they
didn't want to break anything.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.040
But this exposes them to risk.

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:02.010
So we heard our
customers ask for tooling

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.170
to understand this
risk and help them

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:07.400
get towards a state
of least privilege.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:11.330
Another big problem in the
cloud is human misconfiguration.

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:14.480
So what was needed was some
advanced tools that admins

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.680
can turn on for the end
users to detect bad policies,

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:21.500
self-diagnosis in a way,
that goes a long way

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.690
to give admins the
power to say it's

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:25.673
not only up to the
individual project teams,

00:03:25.673 --> 00:03:27.840
but I'm going to check for
these bad things as well.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:32.860
So if you're a security
professional who's

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:35.740
concerned about this, here
is some prescriptive guidance

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:38.500
from Google on how you should
think about getting to least

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:40.140
privilege.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.360
As with most things,
start with the why.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:45.520
First, understand
how much access

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:49.000
is provisioned across
your production workloads.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.250
To initiate any kind
of policy cleanup,

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:53.650
you will first need
data that's compelling

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:56.590
and based on actual usage.

00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:59.740
Then tie that clean-up back
to organization's goals,

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:02.320
like containing insider risk,
which is one of the leading

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:05.370
causes of breaches and fines.

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:07.870
And aim towards educating
your project team

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:10.110
so they are better aware
of their risk posture

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:13.580
and they are driving
towards cultural change.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:16.519
Then, as you are ready
to start taking action,

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:20.060
create a phase plan that starts
with reducing permissions

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:22.100
to other safer baseline.

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:26.030
Most customers opt to do
a quarterly access review.

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:29.420
And then for high privilege
roles like owner and editor,

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:31.730
we recommend a
higher cadence, which

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:34.450
could be on a daily basis.

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:37.720
Lastly, you can also
prioritize based on the member.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:39.720
So you should pay attention
to service accounts,

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.280
maybe start there.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.440
And to reduce grants
and service accounts,

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.110
and especially look for
service account impersonation

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:50.160
permissions if they are not
needed for individual members.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:53.330
Also, look for dormant users who
might be orphaned but present

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:54.780
in your policies.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:59.760
And lastly, in terms of who does
this, we feel in order for this

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.410
to be successful with
the right tooling,

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:05.550
least privilege should be the
responsibility of all project

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:08.880
teams, and not just
security professionals.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:12.310
So here's where IAM
Recommender comes in.

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:15.250
It helps security and
nonsecurity professionals

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:17.830
identify and remove
unwanted access

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:22.240
to GCP by using machine learning
to make smart access control

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:23.920
recommendations.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:26.500
Right here in the IAM
page, the Recommender

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:29.620
is going to show you how many
permissions you have given

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:32.440
and what is actually being used.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:36.410
There's no need to collect
and parse through access logs.

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:38.620
We've done all that
hard work for you.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:41.710
In the back-end, we store
and process petabytes of data

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:43.270
on a daily basis.

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:46.600
All of this is at no additional
charge to our customers.

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:48.860
We now simply show
you this information,

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:50.740
show you this compelling
data, right next

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:53.190
to the user's assigned role.

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:55.830
Now, it looks like most
of the users in my project

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:58.147
here are OK.

00:05:58.147 --> 00:05:59.730
And a few of them
are using a lot less

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:01.410
than they were granted.

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:04.680
You will see here that three
light bulbs have popped up.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:06.810
This is where our
ML models have found

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:10.890
a recommendation for a better
fitting role automatically

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:12.000
in the background.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:13.440
I've had to do no work.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.360
The service account
here on the top row

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:18.270
has the owner role, with
about 1,800 permissions

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:21.250
but is only using
31 out of them.

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:22.950
So here, IAM
Recommender shows me

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:27.290
that I must change its role
to reduce security risk.

00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:28.710
Now, if I'm
interested in details,

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:30.960
I can double-click and
get some more analysis

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:32.850
behind the recommendation.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:35.520
So here, the tool is showing me
that the service account should

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:37.890
be moved to the
BigQuery admin role

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:40.380
and why by highlighting
the permissions

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:42.190
that are safe to remove in red.

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:44.190
ML also picks up
that this account

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:46.160
will need the saved
queries update

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:50.380
permission in the future based
on similar usage patterns.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:52.410
Now, this is a
permission it already has

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:53.940
but it has not used.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:56.280
And you can find this
marked with the blue ML

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:58.990
icon everywhere in the
[INAUDIBLE] [? console. ?]

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:02.640
So this helps make sure that
this user or account does not

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:04.920
miss out on permissions
and you don't need to come

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:07.510
back later to request more.

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:09.420
And now, in our
latest update, we

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:11.280
have also added
the ability for you

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:13.530
to customize the recommendation.

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:16.470
So if you want to remove
a permission or add one,

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:18.600
you can do that right
here in the recommendation

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:23.040
screen in context, which
creates a custom role.

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:24.480
And now this custom
role would be

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:28.860
based on actual activity data
and improved with Google ML

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:32.580
to suggest which permissions you
should have based on observed

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:34.420
patterns in the Cloud.

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:37.480
So as a user, you just need to
hit Apply to make the change.

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:39.170
And this custom
role gets created.

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:42.180
And now it should be
recommended to other users who

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:45.590
have the similar need.

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:47.680
Now Uber has taken some
really innovative steps

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:48.863
with our recommendations.

00:07:48.863 --> 00:07:51.280
And they've integrated this
into their security automation

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:52.520
pipeline.

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:54.610
So now to take you through
that journey, Sonal,

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:56.068
we'd love to
understand what you've

00:07:56.068 --> 00:07:59.610
done with our recommendations.

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:01.320
SONAL DESAI: Thank you, Abhi.

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:05.000
Uber has a tripod strategy
for infrastructure.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:10.350
We maintain on-premise and we
have a presence in AWS and GCP.

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:12.630
In GCP, we are a
large-scale shop.

00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:17.040
You can see we have 90,000
role bindings alone.

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:20.620
Our cloud security team is
responsible for security

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:23.160
in AWS and GCP.

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:26.980
Our team's mission is to
enable our partner teams, which

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:29.530
are our business teams,
to conduct business

00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:34.270
as usual and leverage cloud
in a secure and efficient way.

00:08:34.270 --> 00:08:38.570
We are entrusted with functions
like inventory management,

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:42.100
asset management,
roles granting,

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:45.960
and provisioning in the cloud.

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:49.240
At Uber, we would like
builders to build.

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:51.540
With the above
functions in mind,

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:54.360
we would like to strike a
balance between our partner

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:56.590
teams and the security team.

00:08:56.590 --> 00:08:59.310
And it is hard to do
because partner teams want

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:02.160
ease of allocating
resources in the cloud.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:04.450
They want ease of
maintaining their resources.

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:07.260
And we want to encourage
ownership in them.

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:10.080
As a cloud team, we
would like our eyes

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:12.270
to be on our cloud
infrastructure.

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:14.670
We would like to remediate
over-granted permissions,

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:17.490
and overall maintain
least privilege.

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:20.430
And by that, I mean
reduce our attack surface

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:24.100
and apply permissions as needed.

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:28.440
So let's focus on
over-granted permissions.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:31.410
Over-provisioning can
lead to data breaches.

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:35.160
For example, an identity
that has high privilege role

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:39.880
gets compromised and is
used to access PCI PII data.

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:44.040
This is data with high level of
data classification as needed

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:45.090
by compliance.

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:47.160
This can lead to
severe problems.

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:50.430
It is a real-world example
and we have seen it before.

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:52.590
It is quite common.

00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:55.920
Asking engineers to
choose a more nuanced role

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:57.660
is confusing to them.

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:01.350
They often resort in picking
up editor or owner rules

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:04.460
because it gets the work done.

00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:07.020
So then what is our
approach to least privilege?

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:09.190
We would like to
monitor our footprint.

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:10.540
What are our cloud assets?

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:12.310
What are our identities?

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:14.980
How many roles are
for those identities?

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:17.960
We have targeted
use cases in mind.

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:20.440
For example, service account
with primitive roles,

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:22.510
with each of this
roles having more

00:10:22.510 --> 00:10:25.660
than 2,500 permissions,
same for user

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:28.370
accounts and Google Groups.

00:10:28.370 --> 00:10:30.410
Then we have the
orphaned accounts.

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:33.440
For example, a co-worker
has left the company.

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:35.410
And in Uber's case,
the account needs

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:38.380
to be cleaned up
in GCP environment.

00:10:38.380 --> 00:10:41.220
Then there are the older,
unused permissions.

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:42.890
These are not used by anybody.

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:44.990
They are simply lying
around, unacknowledged.

00:10:44.990 --> 00:10:47.380
We would like to clean them up.

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:49.670
With this, we have
two solutions in mind.

00:10:49.670 --> 00:10:52.660
One is create a ticket
for the partner teams

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:55.320
simply because we do not
have enough business context.

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:57.730
In the ticket, we'll
provide enough information

00:10:57.730 --> 00:11:01.600
of why the ticket is generated
and how to go about fixing it.

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:04.450
The second one is to auto
apply the recommendation

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:06.490
by the security team itself.

00:11:06.490 --> 00:11:10.500
And it should be done
in an automated way.

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:12.600
So with the least
privilege in mind,

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:14.340
we would like our
recommendations

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:16.020
to be data-backed.

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:19.200
In our experience with the
previous cloud provider,

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:21.480
we found that we have
to manually write

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:24.780
scripts to find out
the activity log,

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:28.140
data mine the access
pattern, and then

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:30.420
put the data into
sheet, and then

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:32.610
go through each of
the data manually,

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:35.310
and try to remediate
the recommendation.

00:11:35.310 --> 00:11:37.320
It often leads to
problems like access

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:39.750
denied because there is
no other intelligence

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:43.390
for the recommendation than
simply the access traffic.

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:48.270
Enter GCP IAM Recommender,
with all the bells and whistles

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:51.570
of being based on
historical activity logs

00:11:51.570 --> 00:11:55.500
and being backed by ML, wherein
it predicts if a role might

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:58.060
be needed in near future.

00:11:58.060 --> 00:11:59.710
We do not have to
pay for the logs

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:02.590
because IAM Recommender
does all the heavy lifting

00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:06.100
of collecting, storing,
and analyzing logs.

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:08.590
It is also API-driven,
which makes

00:12:08.590 --> 00:12:13.670
it possible to automate and
make it a repeatable process.

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:15.250
So what is our solution?

00:12:15.250 --> 00:12:17.140
This is a very
high level overview

00:12:17.140 --> 00:12:20.230
of how we have automated
IAM Recommender.

00:12:20.230 --> 00:12:23.140
The major components
are manager--

00:12:23.140 --> 00:12:26.290
manager runs periodically
to get recommendations

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:28.840
from IAM recommended API.

00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:30.760
It simplifies the
recommendations

00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:34.710
to be used internally and then
stores it into a database.

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:37.450
It then invokes an action.

00:12:37.450 --> 00:12:40.220
Action is a rules-based engine.

00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:43.030
It determines what
should be the remediation

00:12:43.030 --> 00:12:47.680
step for the recommendation
and it also prioritizes.

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:49.870
Because we have
hundreds of projects

00:12:49.870 --> 00:12:52.000
and we have hundreds
of recommendations

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:54.490
for each of those projects,
it was important for us

00:12:54.490 --> 00:12:57.100
to prioritize the
recommendation.

00:12:57.100 --> 00:13:00.560
And hence, we introduced
the concept of risk coding.

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:04.130
Our risk coding is
based on two steps.

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:08.080
First, business rules-- we
started with the NIST framework

00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:10.450
and then we created
our own business rules

00:13:10.450 --> 00:13:14.300
based on the tech and
business priorities.

00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:18.890
For example, a project that
has on-premise connectivity

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:23.000
or a project that has data
classification levels of PCI,

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:26.870
PII, all the recommendations
for that project

00:13:26.870 --> 00:13:29.970
will be marked with
higher priority.

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:31.970
The second is access type.

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:34.700
Access type is based on
the number of permissions

00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:37.730
and whether the type of
access is edit and delete.

00:13:37.730 --> 00:13:42.710
It will have higher priority
than, say, a view-only role.

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:45.500
The third step is remediate.

00:13:45.500 --> 00:13:47.765
The remediation could
either be create a ticket

00:13:47.765 --> 00:13:49.820
for the partner
team, in which case

00:13:49.820 --> 00:13:51.390
we'll create a [? GR ?] ticket.

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:54.080
Or it could be auto applying
the recommendation, which

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:56.300
is an automated way
that the pipeline kicks

00:13:56.300 --> 00:13:58.830
in and auto-remediates.

00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:03.530
So now let's look into
the detail architecture.

00:14:03.530 --> 00:14:06.800
We wanted to make the
architecture serverless

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:10.820
and event driven based on
Cloud functions and Pub/Sub,

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:15.690
so as to make it scalable and
extensible to future use cases.

00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:20.360
The first step is to
get the recommendations.

00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:23.390
We spoke about
[? how ?] the manager,

00:14:23.390 --> 00:14:25.250
which is a Cloud Function.

00:14:25.250 --> 00:14:28.100
And before I delve
into the detail,

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:31.610
we talked about the serverless,
event-driven architecture

00:14:31.610 --> 00:14:33.770
based on Cloud
Functions and Pub/Sub,

00:14:33.770 --> 00:14:36.140
so as to make it
scalable and extensible

00:14:36.140 --> 00:14:38.180
for future use cases.

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:40.610
[? So ?] a manager, which
is a Cloud Function, runs

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:43.820
periodically, in our
case on a daily basis.

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:46.280
[INAUDIBLE] IAM Recommender
API [? patches ?]

00:14:46.280 --> 00:14:50.180
the recommendations and
stores it in a FireStore DB.

00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:54.380
The reason we chose FireStore
is because it's easy to use

00:14:54.380 --> 00:14:56.900
and we needed a NoSQL
solution [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:56.900 --> 00:15:00.600
extensibility of
the schema in mind.

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:03.400
So then the second
floor is where,

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:06.700
using data transfer jobs,
we get recommendations

00:15:06.700 --> 00:15:10.090
from IAM Recommender and
stored it into the BigQuery.

00:15:10.090 --> 00:15:14.170
BigQuery allows us to have
a single pane of glass

00:15:14.170 --> 00:15:19.080
that we could see all the
permissions across Uber are.

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:23.010
The second component in
the first step is Enricher.

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:25.120
It has two main functions.

00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:28.070
It gets project details
from Resource Manager.

00:15:28.070 --> 00:15:29.620
And the project
details could be who

00:15:29.620 --> 00:15:32.100
is the owner of the
project, or what was

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:34.140
the create time of the project.

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:37.440
And the second details
it gets is from G Suite.

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:41.220
And it gets details about the
active employees in the company

00:15:41.220 --> 00:15:42.360
right now.

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:45.120
This is needed for
the orphan use case.

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:48.810
All these details are
stored in FireStore DB.

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:54.820
Then step two is
where the manager

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:57.790
invokes an action via Pub/Sub.

00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:02.540
What is the action doing?

00:16:02.540 --> 00:16:06.350
The action [INAUDIBLE] the
recommendation and determines

00:16:06.350 --> 00:16:09.590
what is the next step, or
what is the right remediation,

00:16:09.590 --> 00:16:12.080
to apply to that recommendation.

00:16:12.080 --> 00:16:14.810
It also decides
what is the priority

00:16:14.810 --> 00:16:16.320
and the risk [? call. ?]

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:18.950
So that is where we do
prioritizing and actioning

00:16:18.950 --> 00:16:22.460
on each recommendation
in the FireStore DB.

00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:27.200
Once this is done, we move on to
step three, which is remediate.

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:30.620
So in step three, if
it is a create a ticket

00:16:30.620 --> 00:16:33.230
type of recommendation,
then the action

00:16:33.230 --> 00:16:35.460
invokes an internal service.

00:16:35.460 --> 00:16:38.570
The internal services reads the
details of the recommendation

00:16:38.570 --> 00:16:41.880
from FireStore DB and
creates a [? GR ?] ticket.

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:45.280
If it's an auto apply
type of recommendation,

00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:48.750
then the action invokes
remediator, which

00:16:48.750 --> 00:16:50.310
is basically a cloud function.

00:16:50.310 --> 00:16:53.730
And the remediator reads the
details from FireStore DB,

00:16:53.730 --> 00:16:55.620
and it does then two steps.

00:16:55.620 --> 00:17:00.120
One, it calls IAM Recommender
to claim the recommendation.

00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:04.560
And second, it calls Cloud IAM
to apply that recommendation

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:06.839
and mark it successful.

00:17:06.839 --> 00:17:10.140
Please note, like
we said before,

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:13.500
apply the entire recommendation
as a whole policy,

00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:16.920
and not just a
specific role binding.

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:20.480
Now, I would love to show
you how we have built it.

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:23.900
This is the Cloud Console
where our project teams can

00:17:23.900 --> 00:17:26.810
go and see what
are the IAM roles

00:17:26.810 --> 00:17:28.500
and what are the
recommendations.

00:17:28.500 --> 00:17:31.460
For example, the first
one that you can see

00:17:31.460 --> 00:17:33.590
is a service icon
account for compute.

00:17:33.590 --> 00:17:36.020
And it's been assigned
an editor role.

00:17:36.020 --> 00:17:38.810
And that is an icon
from IAM Recommender

00:17:38.810 --> 00:17:41.810
to suggest there is a
recommendation for it.

00:17:41.810 --> 00:17:44.900
Next, our step
one has kicked in.

00:17:44.900 --> 00:17:48.470
And the manager calls
IAM Recommender API

00:17:48.470 --> 00:17:51.810
to get payload about
the recommendations.

00:17:51.810 --> 00:17:54.330
This is what a typical
payload looks like.

00:17:54.330 --> 00:17:57.320
As you can see, there are
two actions being suggested.

00:17:57.320 --> 00:17:59.390
One is to remove the role.

00:17:59.390 --> 00:18:01.820
And one is to add a role.

00:18:01.820 --> 00:18:04.130
Also note, there
is an E tag, which

00:18:04.130 --> 00:18:07.670
we use for concurrency
and versioning control.

00:18:07.670 --> 00:18:10.520
Each time there is an
update to a recommendation,

00:18:10.520 --> 00:18:12.720
a new E tag is generated.

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:14.150
Now comes step two.

00:18:14.150 --> 00:18:17.240
This is where the manager has
simplified the recommendation

00:18:17.240 --> 00:18:18.650
for internal use.

00:18:18.650 --> 00:18:21.050
You can very clearly
see the action

00:18:21.050 --> 00:18:23.810
is to remove the editor
role and to add a smaller

00:18:23.810 --> 00:18:25.610
Dataproc worker role.

00:18:25.610 --> 00:18:28.543
And you can see the action has
also given us a [? reality, ?]

00:18:28.543 --> 00:18:30.710
which is medium, and a risk
[? course. ?] So we have

00:18:30.710 --> 00:18:32.390
prioritized it.

00:18:32.390 --> 00:18:35.600
Now, the action has determined
this particular case

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:38.600
is for create a ticket
type remediation.

00:18:38.600 --> 00:18:40.860
And that's why a [? GR ?]
ticket is created.

00:18:40.860 --> 00:18:42.470
This is a sample ticket here.

00:18:42.470 --> 00:18:44.540
But some things to
note, the ticket

00:18:44.540 --> 00:18:46.460
is assigned to
the project owner.

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:50.060
In this case, it is me
because I own the project.

00:18:50.060 --> 00:18:53.780
We also include a resource
link so that project owners

00:18:53.780 --> 00:18:55.520
can directly click
on the link and go

00:18:55.520 --> 00:18:59.060
to GCP Console, where they can
see the actual recommendation.

00:18:59.060 --> 00:19:01.190
Then we have the
description, wherein

00:19:01.190 --> 00:19:03.740
we note why this
ticket was generated

00:19:03.740 --> 00:19:07.160
and how to go about fixing
it with detailed screenshots

00:19:07.160 --> 00:19:08.340
and steps.

00:19:08.340 --> 00:19:12.880
There is also a security level
and order priority level.

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:16.910
Coming to step three, this
is where a project owner

00:19:16.910 --> 00:19:20.720
can go in the Cloud Console
and apply the remediation.

00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:22.620
You can see from
our previous steps

00:19:22.620 --> 00:19:25.400
this is the same service
account for compute example.

00:19:25.400 --> 00:19:27.320
The recommendation
is applied and now,

00:19:27.320 --> 00:19:30.410
instead of editor role, it
has the Dataproc worker role,

00:19:30.410 --> 00:19:35.140
and there is no IAM Recommender
sign of [INAUDIBLE]..

00:19:35.140 --> 00:19:38.660
So lessons learned--
as with any product,

00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:42.470
it is important to spend
time upfront in understanding

00:19:42.470 --> 00:19:46.150
the APIs and trust
the recommendations

00:19:46.150 --> 00:19:48.220
or trust the data.

00:19:48.220 --> 00:19:50.670
Our technical
design and approach

00:19:50.670 --> 00:19:53.130
came about from the
proof of concept

00:19:53.130 --> 00:19:55.080
we did right in the beginning.

00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:59.490
It was very easy to auto-apply
all the recommendations we got.

00:19:59.490 --> 00:20:00.930
But then we found
that there were

00:20:00.930 --> 00:20:05.100
edge cases like backups or
break glass accounts, where

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:07.860
we needed manual intervention.

00:20:07.860 --> 00:20:11.190
Learning the landscape--
we knew we had a problem

00:20:11.190 --> 00:20:12.930
with over-granted permissions.

00:20:12.930 --> 00:20:15.780
But we did not know how
severe the problem was.

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:18.870
BigQuery and data
transfer jobs helped

00:20:18.870 --> 00:20:22.410
us get a cross-project
overview across the arc

00:20:22.410 --> 00:20:25.170
and understand
our IAM footprint.

00:20:25.170 --> 00:20:27.630
[INAUDIBLE] with a better
understanding of our IAM

00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:31.750
status, it solidified our intent
in going after our IAM access

00:20:31.750 --> 00:20:35.440
and using IAM Recommender.

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:37.780
And finally, being
early adopters,

00:20:37.780 --> 00:20:40.210
we got stuck at times.

00:20:40.210 --> 00:20:42.980
Here, we were able to leverage
our partnership with Google

00:20:42.980 --> 00:20:47.440
in terms of technical expertise
and product assistance.

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:49.720
It was a great overall
learning experience.

00:20:49.720 --> 00:20:52.240
And Google was an
awesome partner.

00:20:52.240 --> 00:20:52.772
Thank you.

00:20:52.772 --> 00:20:53.980
ABHI YADAV: Thank you, Sonal.

00:20:53.980 --> 00:20:57.100
I'm so happy to hear that
and share your story.

00:20:57.100 --> 00:21:00.340
Super excited about customers
like Uber and thousands

00:21:00.340 --> 00:21:02.620
more who are using IAM
Recommender every day

00:21:02.620 --> 00:21:05.650
to minimize their attack
surface and achieve least

00:21:05.650 --> 00:21:07.660
privileged with least effort.

00:21:07.660 --> 00:21:09.970
We introduced this
product last year at Next

00:21:09.970 --> 00:21:12.310
to provide AI for
cloud governance.

00:21:12.310 --> 00:21:15.610
And since then, we have launched
several new and interesting

00:21:15.610 --> 00:21:17.270
features.

00:21:17.270 --> 00:21:21.010
First, we have made policy
utilization data more readily

00:21:21.010 --> 00:21:23.620
accessible with IAM Insights.

00:21:23.620 --> 00:21:25.600
This lets you build
custom reports

00:21:25.600 --> 00:21:29.110
or integrate recommendations
into your governance tools.

00:21:29.110 --> 00:21:31.540
Second, we now
support recommendation

00:21:31.540 --> 00:21:35.170
of multiple granular roles to
help you migrate off legacy

00:21:35.170 --> 00:21:37.530
roles like owner and editor.

00:21:37.530 --> 00:21:40.570
Next, we have expanded our
analysis and recommendations

00:21:40.570 --> 00:21:41.700
to groups.

00:21:41.700 --> 00:21:44.200
So what we will now tell you,
if a group is over-provisioned

00:21:44.200 --> 00:21:46.820
and what role to assign it.

00:21:46.820 --> 00:21:49.550
Lastly, we not recommend
creation of custom roles

00:21:49.550 --> 00:21:51.960
where, if you find
that a custom role can

00:21:51.960 --> 00:21:54.610
be used across your projects to
reduce over-granting of access,

00:21:54.610 --> 00:21:57.850
we take you through the steps
of creating that custom role.

00:21:57.850 --> 00:22:00.370
And we have many more
features in development

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:02.830
that our customers are
pretty excited about.

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:06.370
Another area we have invested
in is making recommendations

00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:07.270
easier to discover.

00:22:07.270 --> 00:22:10.440
We felt this is an area that
needed much more investment.

00:22:10.440 --> 00:22:13.390
Now project teams can
self-serve in the Cloud Console

00:22:13.390 --> 00:22:16.640
in the IAM page, as
you have seen already.

00:22:16.640 --> 00:22:19.460
And now they're also able
to find recommendations

00:22:19.460 --> 00:22:22.880
in a recommendation hub that
is located on the Cloud Console

00:22:22.880 --> 00:22:24.550
dashboard.

00:22:24.550 --> 00:22:27.280
Here, project teams
can review the insights

00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:28.990
and recommendations
and apply them

00:22:28.990 --> 00:22:31.280
if they have the permission.

00:22:31.280 --> 00:22:33.930
For central security teams,
we now support out-of-the-box

00:22:33.930 --> 00:22:35.760
integration with BigQuery.

00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:38.880
With a few simple steps, you can
export all your recommendations

00:22:38.880 --> 00:22:40.410
to a BigQuery table.

00:22:40.410 --> 00:22:44.350
And this would allow you to
create org-wide custom reports.

00:22:44.350 --> 00:22:47.040
For example, if you want
a view of all provisioning

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:51.030
across the production
workloads, or analyze

00:22:51.030 --> 00:22:53.760
permissions for a
given service account,

00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:58.280
you can now do this easily
without analytical tools.

00:22:58.280 --> 00:23:02.000
Lastly, we exposed insights and
recommendations through APIs

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:05.780
with methods such as Get,
Claim, Mark as Succeeded.

00:23:05.780 --> 00:23:08.300
So in addition to
automating recommendations,

00:23:08.300 --> 00:23:11.030
as we heard Uber describe
today, some customers

00:23:11.030 --> 00:23:14.000
have used this to create
alerts and reminders

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:16.640
such as weekly digests that
they send their project

00:23:16.640 --> 00:23:21.200
teams to alert them they
need to take action.

00:23:21.200 --> 00:23:23.230
We hope you've
enjoyed this deep dive

00:23:23.230 --> 00:23:25.330
into IAM Recommender today.

00:23:25.330 --> 00:23:27.700
We are excited to
announce that Recommender

00:23:27.700 --> 00:23:31.360
is a part of Active Assist, a
brand new solution from Google

00:23:31.360 --> 00:23:33.780
Cloud that we've just announced.

00:23:33.780 --> 00:23:35.530
Active Assist is a
new solution that

00:23:35.530 --> 00:23:38.680
focuses on reducing complexity
in your cloud environment

00:23:38.680 --> 00:23:42.700
by providing you with a variety
of intelligent and proactive

00:23:42.700 --> 00:23:47.350
features, many of which are
powered by machine learning.

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:50.230
By using the capabilities
within Active Assist,

00:23:50.230 --> 00:23:54.160
you are empowered to focus more
of your time on your business

00:23:54.160 --> 00:23:57.950
and less on cloud
administration.

00:23:57.950 --> 00:24:01.220
By using the intelligent
capabilities of Active Assist,

00:24:01.220 --> 00:24:03.380
it makes it really
easy and efficient

00:24:03.380 --> 00:24:05.810
for your teams to do
their jobs, especially

00:24:05.810 --> 00:24:09.170
with things like finding
out what went wrong quickly

00:24:09.170 --> 00:24:12.380
so you can take action right
away with insightful, effective

00:24:12.380 --> 00:24:14.270
troubleshooting tools.

00:24:14.270 --> 00:24:17.360
Secondly, preventing mistakes
from happening altogether

00:24:17.360 --> 00:24:20.210
by relying on analysis
tools before you

00:24:20.210 --> 00:24:21.910
can make the changes.

00:24:21.910 --> 00:24:25.130
And lastly, as we saw today,
improving your cloud easily

00:24:25.130 --> 00:24:27.650
with fast, actionable
recommendations

00:24:27.650 --> 00:24:31.200
with clear rationales for why.

00:24:31.200 --> 00:24:32.910
To get started
with Active Assist,

00:24:32.910 --> 00:24:35.670
we encourage you to
visit the website

00:24:35.670 --> 00:24:38.220
and go over to Cloud Console,
to the recommendation

00:24:38.220 --> 00:24:41.130
hub on the IAM page, and see if
there are any recommendations

00:24:41.130 --> 00:24:43.030
there waiting for you.

00:24:43.030 --> 00:24:44.500
Some of the features
we highlighted

00:24:44.500 --> 00:24:46.840
are only available
in preview today.

00:24:46.840 --> 00:24:49.210
But if you are interested in
getting early access to all

00:24:49.210 --> 00:24:52.340
of these features as they get
developed and give us feedback,

00:24:52.340 --> 00:24:55.000
we encourage you to join
our Insider Group, which

00:24:55.000 --> 00:24:57.010
is linked on our website.

00:24:57.010 --> 00:25:00.190
And finally, check out these
other Cloud OnAir sessions

00:25:00.190 --> 00:25:03.520
for deeper dive into
some of these features.

00:25:03.520 --> 00:25:04.520
Thank you for your time.

00:25:04.520 --> 00:25:06.103
We hope you enjoyed
our session today.

00:25:06.103 --> 00:25:07.970
And happy IAMing.

